---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { REPO } from '../../lib/site';
// The files below are the ones in docs/integrations/ that
// lab/integration/log_consumers.sh runs in the real tools; the page shows them
// without their header comments, so it cannot drift from what was tested.
import f2bFilterRaw from '../../../../docs/integrations/fail2ban/filter.d/howdah.conf?raw';
import f2bJailRaw from '../../../../docs/integrations/fail2ban/jail.d/howdah.conf?raw';
import csParserRaw from '../../../../docs/integrations/crowdsec/parsers/s01-parse/howdah-logs.yaml?raw';
import csScenarioRaw from '../../../../docs/integrations/crowdsec/scenarios/howdah-blocked.yaml?raw';
import csAcquisRaw from '../../../../docs/integrations/crowdsec/acquis.d/howdah.yaml?raw';

const body = (text: string) => text.replace(/^(#[^\n]*\n)+\n*/, '').trimEnd();
const f2bFilter = body(f2bFilterRaw);
const f2bJail = body(f2bJailRaw);
const csParser = body(csParserRaw);
const csScenario = body(csScenarioRaw);
const csAcquis = body(csAcquisRaw);

const INT = `${REPO}/tree/main/docs/integrations`;
const README = `${REPO}/blob/main/docs/integrations/README.md`;
const LAB = `${REPO}/blob/main/lab/integration/log_consumers.sh`;

const nginx = String.raw`set_real_ip_from 10.0.0.5;      # your proxy or load balancer; never 0.0.0.0/0
real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For;
real_ip_recursive on;`;
const apache = String.raw`RemoteIPHeader X-Forwarded-For
RemoteIPInternalProxy 10.0.0.5`;
const f2bInstall = String.raw`sudo cp filter.d/howdah.conf /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/
sudo cp jail.d/howdah.conf /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/
sudo fail2ban-regex /var/log/howdah/events.log /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/howdah.conf
sudo fail2ban-client reload
sudo fail2ban-client status howdah`;
const csInstall = String.raw`sudo cp parsers/s01-parse/howdah-logs.yaml /etc/crowdsec/parsers/s01-parse/
sudo cp scenarios/howdah-blocked.yaml /etc/crowdsec/scenarios/
sudo cp acquis.d/howdah.yaml /etc/crowdsec/acquis.d/
sudo cscli explain --file /var/log/howdah/events.log --type howdah
sudo systemctl reload crowdsec
sudo cscli decisions list`;
---
<Layout
  title="Use Howdah's event log with fail2ban, CrowdSec and SIEMs"
  description="Turn requests Howdah refused into firewall bans with fail2ban or CrowdSec, alert on them in Wazuh or Loki, and log the real client address behind a proxy. Every file was run against real event lines."
  path="/docs/integrations/"
>
  <div class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/docs/">Docs</a> / <span aria-current="page">Log integrations</span></p>
      <h1>Use the event log with other tools</h1>
      <p class="lede">Howdah writes one JSON line per event. fail2ban or CrowdSec can turn repeated refusals from one address into a firewall ban, and a SIEM can alert on them. Each file on this page was run against real event lines in the real tool.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="note" role="note" aria-labelledby="scope-heading">
        <h3 id="scope-heading">Before you start</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Only refusals lead to a ban.</strong> A line counts when Howdah refused the request or denied the call (<code>"action":"block"</code>). Detect-mode lines, observe's <code>proposed:</code> lines and <code>excepted</code> lines never count.</li>
          <li><strong>Howdah never touches the firewall</strong> and ships no events off the host itself. The tool that reads the log bans, with its own privileges.</li>
          <li><strong>Behind a proxy, fix the client address first</strong>, or you ban the proxy and with it every visitor.</li>
          <li><strong>The log is only as trustworthy as the PHP worker.</strong> Howdah writes the log from the worker, so code that runs as the worker user after a compromise can add lines and get any address banned. Keep the addresses you must never lose, such as your office and your monitoring, in <code>ignoreip</code> or a CrowdSec allowlist, and keep ban times bounded.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

