      <h2 id="client-address">First: the client address behind a proxy</h2>
      <p><code>client_ip</code> is <code>REMOTE_ADDR</code> as PHP's server API reports it. Howdah never reads <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, because a client can set that header to anything. Behind a reverse proxy, load balancer or CDN, <code>REMOTE_ADDR</code> is the proxy's address.</p>
      <p>Restore the client address in the web server, trusting only your own proxy. nginx in front of php-fpm:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{nginx}</code></pre>
      <p>Apache with mod_php (enable the module with <code>a2enmod remoteip</code> on Debian and Ubuntu):</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{apache}</code></pre>
      <p>The lab checked both. With the module, a request from the trusted hop logs the forwarded address. A request from any other address keeps its own, whatever <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> it sends. Without the module, <code>client_ip</code> is the hop. Also put the proxy in the ban tool's ignore list.</p>

      <h2 id="fail2ban">fail2ban</h2>
      <p>The jail bans an address for an hour after 3 refused requests within 10 minutes, using your default <code>banaction</code>. Set <code>logpath</code> if your log is not <code>/var/log/howdah/events.log</code>, and run the filter against your own log before you reload. From <a href={`${INT}/fail2ban`}>docs/integrations/fail2ban</a>:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{f2bInstall}</code></pre>
      <p><code>filter.d/howdah.conf</code> reads the keys in any order. A request cannot plant a match inside a value, because Howdah writes a quote inside a value as <code>\"</code>.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{f2bFilter}</code></pre>
      <p><code>jail.d/howdah.conf</code>. Its <code>ignoreip</code> replaces any <code>[DEFAULT]</code> one, so add your proxies there and repeat your existing entries:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{f2bJail}</code></pre>
      <p>With <code>[log] destination = "syslog"</code>, point <code>logpath</code> at the file your syslog daemon writes, such as <code>/var/log/syslog</code>. The filter matches the JSON inside those lines too.</p>

      <h2 id="crowdsec">CrowdSec</h2>
      <p>A parser, a scenario and the file acquisition, from <a href={`${INT}/crowdsec`}>docs/integrations/crowdsec</a>. Set the path in the acquisition file, check the parse, then reload:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{csInstall}</code></pre>
      <p>The parser reads each field by name, takes the event time from <code>ts</code> and sets <code>source_ip</code> from <code>client_ip</code>:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{csParser}</code></pre>
      <p>The scenario is a leaky bucket per address, so 3 refused requests in quick succession raise an alert. Your profiles turn the alert into a decision (the default profile bans for 4 hours), and a remediation component (bouncer) applies it:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{csScenario}</code></pre>
      <p>The acquisition, for the event file:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{csAcquis}</code></pre>
      <p>With <code>[log] destination = "syslog"</code>, leave the acquisition file out: your syslog acquisition already reads the lines, and the hub's <code>syslog-logs</code> parser hands them to this one. CrowdSec's <code>crowdsecurity/whitelists</code> parser ignores private and loopback addresses, so this scenario never bans an address in a private range, including an attacker inside your own network.</p>

      <h2 id="siem">Wazuh and Loki</h2>
      <p>For alerts and search rather than bans, the configuration page has a <a href="/docs/configuration/#siem">Wazuh decoder and rules and a Promtail pipeline</a>. Wazuh raises level 10 for <code>block</code> and 5 for <code>log</code>. Promtail labels <code>rule</code>, <code>action</code> and <code>layer</code>. Both are run in the real tools by <code>lab/integration/siem_snippets.sh</code>.</p>

      <h2 id="exec">Why not the exec hook?</h2>
      <p><code>[log] exec</code> runs a program for each event as the PHP worker user. That user must not be able to change the firewall. Use the hook for notifications, such as a webhook or a chat message, and let a log reader with its own privileges do the banning.</p>

      <h2 id="tested">How it was tested</h2>
      <p><a href={LAB}>lab/integration/log_consumers.sh</a> runs Howdah on PHP 8.3 under php-fpm behind nginx, and under Apache mod_php. It sends a known mix of requests from documentation addresses:</p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>4 refused requests from one address,</li>
        <li>5 detect-mode findings from another,</li>
        <li>4 findings an exception turned off, logged as <code>excepted</code>,</li>
        <li>4 hits on a script that Layer 4 observe logs as <code>proposed</code>,</li>
        <li>4 refused requests whose variable name tries to forge <code>"client_ip":"192.0.2.66"</code>,</li>
        <li>and refused requests through a hop with no real-IP setup and from an untrusted hop.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>It then feeds the 33 real event lines it captured to each tool. All 23 checks passed:</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="What the lab checked (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">What the log-consumer lab checked, per tool</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tool</th><th scope="col">Version</th><th scope="col">Checked</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">nginx realip</th><td>1.27</td><td>A trusted hop logs the forwarded address; an untrusted hop keeps its own; without realip, <code>client_ip</code> is the hop and <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> appears in no line</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Apache mod_remoteip</th><td>2.4</td><td>The same three cases, with mod_php</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">fail2ban</th><td>1.1.0</td><td><code>fail2ban-regex</code> matches exactly the 16 block lines, in the file and in two syslog forms; a running jail bans exactly the two outside addresses with 3 or more blocks</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">CrowdSec</th><td>v1.7.0</td><td>All 33 lines parse, in the file and in two syslog forms; a running agent makes ban decisions for exactly those two addresses</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Neither tool banned the detect-mode, excepted, proposed or forged addresses. fail2ban ignored the loopback hop through <code>ignoreip</code>, and CrowdSec through its whitelist.</p>

      <h2 id="not-covered">Not covered</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><strong>The ban itself.</strong> fail2ban ran with its <code>dummy</code> action, because a container has no firewall to change, and no CrowdSec bouncer was installed. The lab checks who would be banned, not the firewall rule.</li>
        <li><strong>The syslog forms were built by the lab</strong> by adding rsyslog's traditional and RFC 3339 prefixes to the captured lines. fail2ban's journal backend (<code>backend = systemd</code>) was not tested.</li>
        <li><strong>IPv6 client addresses</strong> and CDN headers such as <code>CF-Connecting-IP</code> were not tested.</li>
        <li><strong>One PHP version.</strong> The lab ran on PHP 8.3 only.</li>
        <li><strong>Other tools.</strong> Splunk, Elastic, Graylog and Sentinel have no tested snippet. The line is plain JSON, so their generic JSON input should work, but we have not checked any of them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The full notes are in <a href={README}>docs/integrations/README.md</a>, and the line format is in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/log-schema.md`}>docs/log-schema.md</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

